# The SIGIL toolchain sigil-pi is built and tested against.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# sigil-pi is not self-contained: every tool is forged by a sigil-mcp/sigil-serve
# binary built from a sibling SIGIL checkout ($SIGIL_ROOT, default ../SIGIL).
# Nothing used to record WHICH toolchain. On 2026-07-31 a rebuild of that sibling
# turned the whole suite red (44 failures) with no change to sigil-pi at all, and
# there was no way to tell what had moved. Recording it — and checking it in
# ci.sh — turns a baffling failure into a one-line diagnosis.
#
# WHY TREE HASHES, NOT A COMMIT SHA
# SIGIL commits to bench/, docs/ and rl_tasks/ constantly; none of that can change
# the forge binary. A commit pin trips on every one of them (it did, within three
# minutes of being written) and would train everyone to ignore it. These are the
# git tree hashes of the only two directories the binary is built from, so the
# check fires if and only if the toolchain itself actually moved.
#   git -C $SIGIL_ROOT rev-parse HEAD:crates HEAD:stdlib
#
# FORMAT: one `key = value` per line. Blank lines and `#` comments ignored.

crates = 36a698a833f58b752e94cd13d8c4b57d05a77635
stdlib = c8036abf2297a55fee3269fff83cddc6efec8a30

# Human-readable note shown when the check fails.
note = claude/agentic-bench @ 2026-07-31; most-sound taint checker to date

# A commit that CONTAINS the trees above. Convenience only — CI checks this out
# as a candidate and the tree hashes above remain the authority. Local commits
# are fine for local use, but CI can only fetch what is PUSHED.
#
# NOT PUSHED AS OF 2026-07-31: 98681033 exists only in the local checkout, and no
# ref on origin carries the pinned crates/ tree. Until the branch is pushed the
# `forge` job in .github/workflows/ci.yml cannot run, and it skips rather than
# failing red.
ref = 98681033

# KNOWN ISSUE AT THIS TOOLCHAIN (tracked, not accepted):
#   The `json` stdlib declares its parameters with no taint annotation, so they
#   default to @Public. parse_reply.sigil and chat_turn.sigil feed them @Internal
#   HTTP-response data, which the (correctly) tightened checker rejects as a
#   downgrade:
#       T001: cannot pass @Internal value to function `json::array_len`
#             parameter `input_ptr` declared @Public without declassification
#   Parsing untrusted network JSON is that function's entire purpose, so the fix
#   belongs upstream: `json::` needs to be taint-POLYMORPHIC (output label follows
#   input label), not @Public. Until that lands, the agentic-loop tests are red.
#
#   Do NOT work around it with declassify() at sigil-pi's call sites: parse_reply
#   is the zero-grant inner-ring component the security story leans on, and an
#   explicit downgrade there would hollow it out.
#
#   Pinning BACK to a toolchain from before 2026-07-21 (f7fefd8f "Refine soundness
#   enforcement") makes the suite green again and keeps the 2026-07-11 redirect
#   hardening (a4b65313). But it also gives up the taint-soundness fixes that
#   closed fail-open T001 holes (16f93fd2, bc1cfe25, 1eb5d516) — i.e. it would run
#   this project's central non-leakage proof on a checker with known holes. That
#   is a deliberate trade, not a default; change the hashes above only on purpose.
